{{Short description|History taken verbally and recorded or transcribed}}
{{About|the academic discipline of collecting firsthand memories of historical importance|the use of oral traditions as historical sources|Oral tradition#Historiography}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=November 2024}}
[[File:Oral history baltimore.jpg|thumb|An Evergreen Protective Association volunteer recording an oral history at Greater Rosemont History Day]]
'''Oral history''' is the collection and study of historical information from people, families, important events, or everyday life by using [[audiotapes]], [[videotapes]], or transcriptions of planned [[interviews]]. The interviews are conducted with people who participated in or observed past events and whose memories and perceptions of these are to be preserved as an aural record for future generations. Oral history strives to obtain information from different perspectives and most of these cannot be found in written sources.

Oral history also refers to information gathered in that manner and to a written work (published or unpublished) based on such data, which is often preserved in [[archives]] and large [[libraries]].<ref name="Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia 2013">oral history. (n.d.) The Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia®. (2013). Retrieved 24 September 2026 from https://web.archive.org/web/20180313092344/https://encyclopedia2.thefreedictionary.com/oral+history</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.abc-clio.com/ODLIS/odlis_o.aspx#oralhistory |title=Definition of ''oral history'' from the Online Dictionary for Library and Information Science}}</ref><ref>{{Cite American Heritage Dictionary|oral history}}</ref><ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |url=http://www.lexico.com/definition/oral_history |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200322182059/https://www.lexico.com/definition/oral_history |url-status=dead |archive-date=22 March 2020 |title=oral history |dictionary=[[Lexico]] UK English Dictionary |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]}}</ref> Knowledge presented by oral history is unique in that it shares the tacit perspective, thoughts, opinions, and understanding of the interviewee in its primary form.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Nkala |first1=Gugulethu Shamaine |last2=David |first2=Rodreck |title=Oral History Sources as Learning Materials: A Case Study of The National University of Science and Technology |journal=Oral History Journal of South Africa |year=2015 |volume=3 |issue=2 |pages=82 |doi=10.25159/2309-5792/340 |doi-broken-date=15 January 2026 |url=https://unisapressjournals.co.za/index.php/OHJSA/article/view/340/468 |access-date=24 September 2026}}</ref>

The term is sometimes used in a more general sense to refer to the study of information about past events that witnesses told anybody else,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.macmillandictionary.com/dictionary/american/oral-history |title=Definition of ''oral history'' from the Macmillan Dictionary}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/british/oral-history |title=Definition of ''oral history'' from the Cambridge Advanced Learner's Dictionary}}</ref> but professional [[historians]] usually consider this to be the study of [[oral tradition]] or ''traditional oral history'' because the source receives the information aurally.

{{Short description|Group of individuals of a species, separated from other groups by in some manner}}
{{About-distinguish|the biology concept|Population (human biology)}}
{{pp-move}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=November 2024}}

In [[biology]], a '''population''' of [[Organism|organisms]] is a group of individuals of the same [[species]], defined by a discontinuity or [[wikt:disjunction|disjunction]] from other groups of individuals in certain characteristics, such as living area, genetic attributes, demographic structure.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last1=Wells |first1=Jeffrey V. |last2=Richmond |first2=Milo E. |date=1995 |title=Populations, Metapopulations, and Species Populations: What Are They and Who Should Care? |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281243816 |journal=Wildlife Society Bulletin |volume=23 |issue=3 |pages=458–462 |issn=0091-7648 |jstor=3782955}}</ref> Among biologists, the term definition varies, in some cases significantly, and sometimes those variations can be confusing.<ref name=":1" /> There are also plenty of other terms to describe groups of individuals if no clear [[wikt:disjunction|disjunction]] is present.<ref name=":1" /> Commonly, a population can be described by what individuals constitute the population, its size, a geographical area it occupies, and the time within which the population is examined.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rastogi |first1=V. B. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XnM4q8nBeWUC |title=A Complete Course in ISC Biology |last2=Kishore |first2=B. |date=1997 |publisher=Pitambar Publishing |isbn=978-81-209-0822-2 |pages=19–21 |language=en}}</ref> In [[wikt:qualitative|qualitative]] terms, it is usually defined like "a group of organisms of the same species occupying a particular space at a particular time".<ref>{{Cite book |last=Krebs |first=Charles J. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=WjGupYHvthUC |title=Ecology: The Experimental Analysis of Distribution and Abundance |date=1972 |publisher=Harper & Row |isbn=978-0-06-043770-1 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":3" />

The two main approaches to define a population are ecological and evolutionary. From the ecological perspective, individuals are considered interacting and competing in a certain geographic area. From the evolutionary (genetic) perspective, genes and reproduction are considered the driving forces of a population.<ref name=":3">{{Cite journal |last1=Waples |first1=Robin S. |last2=Gaggiotti |first2=Oscar |date=2006 |title=INVITED REVIEW: What is a population? An empirical evaluation of some genetic methods for identifying the number of gene pools and their degree of connectivity |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-294X.2006.02890.x |journal=Molecular Ecology |language=en |volume=15 |issue=6 |pages=1419–1439 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-294X.2006.02890.x |pmid=16629801 |bibcode=2006MolEc..15.1419W |issn=1365-294X}}</ref> Since each population has its own gene pool that changes and adapts to the environment over time, the population is considered to be the main organizational unit in biology.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Young |first=Allen M. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yGH1BwAAQBAJ&dq=%22population+is%22+census+biology&pg=PA22 |title=Population Biology of Tropical Insects |date=2012-12-06 |publisher=Springer Science & Business Media |isbn=978-1-4684-1113-3 |pages=22 |language=en}}</ref>

